1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,089
Here is a library request slip.

2
00:00:02,129 --> 00:00:05,660
You write one title, the librarian fetches one
book.

3
00:00:07,733 --> 00:00:10,144
Now someone writes "any book, all of them".

4
00:00:10,184 --> 00:00:14,818
A librarian who copies the slip straight onto the
order fetches the whole library.

5
00:00:21,267 --> 00:00:24,120
The lab's database tool works like that slip.

6
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:27,527
It pastes whatever filter it is given into its
query.

7
00:00:30,933 --> 00:00:31,567
Press Run.

8
00:00:31,607 --> 00:00:34,193
The SQL line shows what the tool built.

9
00:00:34,233 --> 00:00:38,368
One equals one is always true, so the query
matches every row.

10
00:00:42,533 --> 00:00:47,738
Three rows: the whole customer table, from a
perfectly valid query. Nothing broke.

11
00:00:47,778 --> 00:00:50,168
The tool did exactly what it was told.

12
00:00:54,133 --> 00:00:58,967
The fix is a different slip: one box, for exactly
one customer number.

13
00:00:59,007 --> 00:01:02,518
In secure mode the tool refuses a free-form
filter.

14
00:01:07,667 --> 00:01:12,670
Unlike a library, a database runs the slip as
code, instantly and silently.

15
00:01:12,710 --> 00:01:15,427
Nobody looks at the trolley and asks why.

16
00:01:17,333 --> 00:01:21,668
Run it yourself, read your own timeline, then
switch it to secure.
